------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART A: e(y) := D/(x+y) exactly  =>  h(y):=A(y)/(x+y) converges
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
h(y) closed form: -D/(x + y) + D/(Y0 + x) + h_Y0
lim_{y->infinity} h(y) = D/(Y0 + x) + h_Y0
[PASS] closed-form limit matches h(Y0) + D/(x+Y0) exactly  -- got D/(Y0 + x) + h_Y0, expected D/(Y0 + x) + h_Y0

Concrete instance: x=1.0, D=2.0, Y0=1.0, h(Y0)=0.3
Predicted limit L = h(Y0)+D/(x+Y0) = 1.3

  Y            h(Y) via mpmath quad of h'        h(Y) closed form        |diff|
  2            0.633333333333333            0.633333333333333        0.0
  5            0.966666666666667            0.966666666666667        1.336e-51
  10           1.11818181818182             1.11818181818182         0.0
  100          1.28019801980198             1.28019801980198         0.0
  1000         1.298001998002               1.298001998002           0.0
  100000       1.2999800002                 1.2999800002             2.138e-50
  1e+07        1.29999980000002             1.29999980000002         1.93e-48
[PASS] independent mpmath quadrature matches closed form to <1e-30  -- max diff = 1.9297361206865652565134673292833699165045721474358e-48

  h(Y=1e+07) = 1.29999980000002, predicted limit L = 1.3, gap = 2.0e-7
[PASS] h(Y) approaches predicted limit L as Y grows (gap < 1e-6 at Y=1e7)  -- gap=0.00000019999998000000199999980000001999999800000019975375
[PASS] Phi_y(x):=h(y)+e(y) also converges to the same L (consistency)  -- Phi_Y=1.3, L=1.3

==> PART A CONFIRMS: an e(y) rate of exactly O(1/z) (the rate this
    front's Sec 3 derives from (B)+(C')+(U)) genuinely suffices for
    h(y)=A(y)/(x+y) -- and hence Phi_y(x) -- to converge.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART B: h(y):=sin(log(log(x+y+3))) -- e(y)=o(1) but NOT O(1/z);
        h(y) does NOT converge (explicit constructive subsequence)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
d/dw[sin(log(log(w)))] = cos(log(log(w)))/(w*log(w))
[PASS] derivative matches cos(log(log(w)))/(w*log(w)) exactly  -- got cos(log(log(w)))/(w*log(w))
Antiderivative of 1/(w*log(w)): log(log(w))
[PASS] antiderivative of the majorant is log(log(w)) (unbounded as w->oo)  -- got log(log(w))
[PASS] log(log(w)) -> infinity as w -> infinity (confirms NON-integrability)  -- got oo

e(y) [as a function of w=x+y+3] = cos(log(log(w))) / log(w)
[PASS] |e(y)| <= 1/log(w) -> 0 as w->infinity (confirms e(y)=o(1))  -- got 0
[PASS] [1/log(w)] / [1/w] -> infinity (1/log(w) is strictly WEAKER than O(1/w))  -- got oo

Constructive non-convergence: for target values v in {-1, 0, 1},
choose theta=asin(v)+2*pi*k (or pi-asin(v)+2*pi*k) and set
y_k := exp(exp(theta)) - 3 (x=0). Then h(y_k) = sin(log(log(y_k+3)))
= sin(theta) = v EXACTLY, by construction, for arbitrarily large y_k.
  target=+1, k=0: theta=1.5708, y_k~exp(exp(1.571)) [order-of-magnitude huge], h(y_k)=1.0, |h(y_k)-target|=0.0  [OK]
  target=+1, k=1: theta=7.85398, y_k~exp(exp(7.854)) [order-of-magnitude huge], h(y_k)=1.0, |h(y_k)-target|=0.0  [OK]
