  target=+0, k=3: theta=18.8496, y_k~exp(exp(18.85)) [order-of-magnitude huge], h(y_k)=1.6755233265582882452e-50, |h(y_k)-target|=1.676e-50  [OK]
[PASS] constructive subsequence hits target values -1,0,+1 exactly, arbitrarily far out (k=0..3 each)
[PASS] y_k strictly increasing across k (genuinely 'arbitrarily far out')  -- y_k0 order 2.07842, y_k1 order 1118.73 (log10)

==> PART B CONFIRMS: weakening the self-averaging error's rate from
    O(1/z) (Part A, sufficient) to the strictly weaker O(1/log z)
    (still o(1), i.e. still consistent with the UNCONDITIONAL
    self-averaging identity of record) is enough to break convergence
    of h(y)=A(y)/(x+y) -- explicit, constructive, not merely sampled.
    This is the SAME qualitative phenomenon as the predecessor's
    sin(log(1+t)) counter-example (wave 26, Sec 6.2), now located
    precisely relative to THIS front's O(1/z) sufficient threshold:
    the threshold is not an arbitrary safety margin, it is close to
    the actual boundary of what works.

